## Issues

### Active (alignment pass — post-move drift)

- [ ] `src/service/install/scaffoldCommands/.aide` frontmatter
  `scope: src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands`
  Stale path — module moved to `src/service/install/scaffoldCommands/`.
  Traces to: physical move; ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` ->
  outcomes.desired[4] (folder tree as architecture).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/scaffoldCommands/.aide` Good examples:
  "A host project runs `aide_init` for the first time..."
  Identity drift — examples narrate the MCP tool path. With two
  consumers (MCP tool and npx CLI) the examples should narrate the
  service contract once or distinguish the two consumer shapes
  explicitly.
  Traces to: parent `src/service/install/.aide` identity drift;
  ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` -> outcomes.desired[1] (two delivery
  channels).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

### Resolved (prior QA cycle — kept for retro context)

- [x] `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/.aide` outcomes.desired[0]: "One slash command file is scaffolded per pipeline phase: research, spec, synthesize, plan, build, qa, fix, plus the update-playbook maintenance command"
  The actual COMMANDS array in scaffoldCommands/index.ts includes 13 entries: aide (orchestrator), research, spec, synthesize, plan, build, qa, fix, upgrade, init, update-playbook, refactor, align. The spec's enumeration omits the orchestrator, upgrade, init, refactor, and align. The spec should enumerate the full set or describe the enumeration mechanism without a hardcoded count.
  Traces to: .aide/docs/index.md -> Pipeline Agents table (eight agents = eight phases including align and refactor); .aide/intent.aide -> outcomes.desired[1] (two delivery channels covering the full lifecycle)
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/.aide` Good examples section: status report shows "the eight pipeline phase commands plus the update-playbook maintenance command"
  The actual scaffolded set is 13 commands. The good example's status report shape no longer matches what the tool actually produces.
  Traces to: .aide/intent.aide -> outcomes.undesired[3] (a canonical doc that describes behavior the server does not actually deliver)
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [x] `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/.aide` Strategy section: "research, spec, synthesize, plan, build, qa, fix, plus the update-playbook maintenance command"
  Strategy's fixed-list description omits the same five commands as the outcomes. The "eight" count in the strategy ("the other six commands also report exists") is also stale.
  Traces to: .aide/docs/index.md -> Pipeline Agents table (canonical pipeline surface)
  Misalignment: spec-gap

## Retro

Two layers of drift in this spec:

1. **Architectural-shift drift (current):** Folder rename plus CLI
   consumer addition should have triggered a `/aide:spec` pass. Examples
   still narrate the MCP-tool agent path.

2. **Prior cycle (resolved):** The command-roster enumeration drift was
   a recurring pattern — every time a new command was added to the
   COMMANDS array, the spec's enumeration in outcomes/Strategy/Good
   examples drifted silently. This is the canonical example of
   `feedback_enumeration_drift.md`: child specs that hardcode counts
   or enumerate by name drift the moment the canonical source changes.
   The post-resolution spec correctly describes coverage abstractly
   ("one-to-one with the registry, no overlap, no extras") and points
   at the registry as the source of truth.
